<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hunald I of Aquitaine</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hunald</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hunald was Duke of Aquitaine from 735, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odo-the-great">Odo the Great</a>. He resisted the authority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a> and his sons Carloman and Pippin the Short, who campaigned against him repeatedly. In 745 he gave up power to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waiofar">Waiofar</a> and retired to a monastery on the Île de Ré.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hunald-i-of-aquitaine.jpg" alt="Hunald I of Aquitaine" width="284" height="257" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Master of the City of Ladies · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hunald.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
